      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wallsburg sprang up in 1861, founded by settlers who carved a path through the rugged Wasatch foothills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallsburg%2C_Utah",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors William Madison Wall, a North Carolina native who helped build the road through Provo Canyon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://onlineutah.us/wallsburghistory.shtml",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wallsburg sits on U‑282, just four miles southeast of Deer Creek Reservoir, making it a quiet agricultural hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://onlineutah.us/wallsburghistory.shtml",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Although small, Wallsburg is officially part of the Salt Lake City metropolitan area, linking it to Utah’s largest city.",
